What is a JOIN?
A JOIN is used to combine rows from two or more tables based on a related column between them (usually a key relationship).

TYPES OF JOINS:
| Type           | Description                                                |
| -------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- |
| **INNER JOIN** | Returns only matching rows in both tables                  |
| **LEFT JOIN**  | Returns all rows from left table + matched rows from right |
| **RIGHT JOIN** | Returns all rows from right table + matched rows from left |
| **FULL JOIN**  | Returns all rows when there’s a match in either table      |
| **CROSS JOIN** | Returns all possible combinations (cartesian product)      |
| **SELF JOIN**  | Joins a table to itself                                    |


LEFT JOIN / RIGHT JOIN : Missing matches show NULLs

FULL JOIN (a.k.a FULL OUTER JOIN): Combines results of LEFT + RIGHT JOIN — shows all records from both tables with NULLs for missing matches.

CROSS JOINS: Returns all combinations of both tables (Cartesian product).
Be careful — output rows = (rows in A × rows in B)

USING Keyword
If column names are identical, you can simplify ON clause using USING().

SELECT e.emp_name, d.dept_name
FROM employees e
JOIN departments d
USING (dept_id);

Above code is same as:
ON e.dept_id = d.dept_id


| Join Type  | Description                        | NULL Behavior               |
| ---------- | ---------------------------------- | --------------------------- |
| INNER JOIN | Only matching rows                 | Excludes non-matching       |
| LEFT JOIN  | All from left + matches from right | NULL for missing right rows |
| RIGHT JOIN | All from right + matches from left | NULL for missing left rows  |
| FULL JOIN  | All from both sides                | NULL on either side         |
| CROSS JOIN | All combinations                   | No condition                |
| SELF JOIN  | Table joins itself                 | Uses alias to differentiate |
